1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,600
If I've told you once,
I've told you a dozen times.

2
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:04,250
Literally.

3
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:05,900
Twelve kinds of time.

4
00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:09,300
Event time. When did it happen?
Apollo 11 landed at 20:17 UTC, July 20, 1969.

5
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:13,400
Valid time. When was it true?
Pluto was a planet from 1930 to 2006. Facts expire.

6
00:00:14,100 --> 00:00:17,500
Record time. When did we find out?
A star exploded around 166,000 BC. Its light was seen in 1987.

7
00:00:18,100 --> 00:00:21,400
Source time. When was it made?
A photo from 2019, downloaded today, is still from 2019. A copy isn't new.

8
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:25,300
Reference time. Which day is "tomorrow"?
Written on Mar 3, it means Mar 4. Read on Mar 9, it seems to mean Mar 10.

9
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:29,400
Sequence time. Which came first?
Two events at 09:00:00 tie. Clocks tie. Order doesn't. (Lamport, 1978)

10
00:00:30,100 --> 00:00:33,300
Verification time. Last checked when?
An elevator passed inspection in Mar 2024. True when checked. Unknown since.

11
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:37,300
Access time. Last looked at when?
Files sorted by last opened. Read most, check first.

12
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:41,300
Volatility. Shelf life?
A status goes stale in 7 days, a decision in 90, a name never. Facts have half-lives.

13
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:45,300
Intended time. When should it happen?
"Remind me next Tuesday." It points forward.

14
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:49,400
Business time. Which calendar?
The UK tax year starts on April 6. A leftover from 1752.

15
00:00:50,100 --> 00:00:53,400
As-of time. From which "now"?
How many planets? As of 2005, nine. As of today, eight.

16
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:56,600
Event, valid, record, source, reference, sequence,
verification, access, volatility, intended, business, as-of.

17
00:00:56,650 --> 00:00:59,500
There. Now I've told you a dozen times.
